# acestream-suite's catalog as an M3U playlist for the house players (VLC, Kodi, Dispatcharr).
#
# Installed by hand on dev.albertucho.home:
#   install -m 0755 scripts/acestream-suite-playlist.py /usr/local/bin/acestream-suite-playlist
#   install -m 0644 scripts/acestream-suite-playlist.cron /etc/cron.d/acestream-suite-playlist
#
# The link it keeps alive is http://192.168.1.245/varios/acestream-suite.m3u, served by the Caddy that
# already serves /srv/www/varios over plain HTTP.
#
# Every fifteen minutes, offset from the hour and the half hour so it never meets a backup:
# what changes between runs is the catalog (channels added or removed in acestream-suite), not the
# streams, and the whole job is one JSON fetch plus one file write. The script writes only when
# the content differs, so an unchanged playlist keeps its mtime and nothing watching the file
# (Dispatcharr refreshes on its own schedule) has a reason to react.
#
# The script takes its own flock and logs to /var/log/acestream-suite-playlist.log, so there is nothing
# to collect here: a run that overlaps the previous one says so in that log and exits.
#
# An alias is where a consumer still asks for another file name: the script copies a list under the
# names in ACESTREAM_SUITE_COMPAT_ALIASES (`new-file=other-file`, comma-separated) and writes nothing
# extra when it is unset. The value lives on the host, not in git, and the line below loads it with
# POSIX sh; the `[ -r ... ]` guard is what keeps the job working on a host without the file.
# The file must EXPORT its values (`export ACESTREAM_SUITE_COMPAT_ALIASES=...`, or `set -a` on
# its first line): a plain assignment in a sourced file is a shell variable and never reaches
# the binary, which is a child process.
SHELL=/bin/bash
PATH=/sbin:/bin:/usr/sbin:/usr/bin:/usr/local/bin
5,20,35,50 * * * * root [ -r /etc/acestream-suite.env ] && . /etc/acestream-suite.env; /usr/local/bin/acestream-suite-playlist >/dev/null 2>&1
